7592186424620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424618 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424620 1069 "q-77145" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424620 1071 "![](images/b3/b3c874f3c4564795a2ddcfb4c2896c1bc0ef1b4b7b3d8e8318f42d15643942d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $UVWU,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424620 1072 #uuid "37832b23-49d2-5d4e-9f43-b03259f479e7" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424620 1074 "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424617 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424621 1069 "q-77258" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424621 1071 "![](images/4d/4d4c2c86ed8506ff034141ac20a5a56587a80370384ceeaf08c66f2ce3e8a315.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(4,-2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(4,-2)$ to the point $B(4,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{8}{3},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424621 1072 #uuid "515517e3-98f2-5a9f-ba03-8d3b47263ffd" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424621 1074 "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the section of the curve $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i-\\sqrt{t}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 4.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=-\\sqrt{t}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j) \\\\  & =t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2})\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{4}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{4}^{3/2}}{3}-0)+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424619 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424622 1069 "q-125414" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424622 1071 "Let $F(x,y,z)={e}^{y+z}\\,i+{e}^{x+z}\\,j+{e}^{x+y}\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424622 1072 #uuid "f7d70870-3ade-5c38-9cda-96260cf64575" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424622 1074 "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424623 1062 #uuid "325a719e-fd77-51b3-8a73-41ee2a5e0859" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424623 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424622 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424624 1058 #edb/ref 17592186424623 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424624 1062 #uuid "7063d477-7b30-59c1-824d-924e55d3b439" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424624 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424621 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424625 1058 #edb/ref 17592186424624 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424625 1062 #uuid "c29adf1a-cc66-5223-9397-edc496c85d37" 13194139534536 17592186045417 true] [17592186424625 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424620 13194139534536 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"activity-c1a363ff-d156-5c04-b913-8176faf9c905" 17592186424601 "answer-09b359e4-02cd-5e97-8b05-35ed5f867cf9" 17592186424602 "answer-23b97760-1703-5852-afc4-69acd21e7cc9" 17592186424603 "answer-2cf6d6b3-ad5e-5dc6-8ebb-24366de741cb" 17592186424604 "answer-4bd6afbc-8569-51f5-a569-dd388d648b11" 17592186424605 "answer-65eebd17-cd71-544b-a441-a52714eab